Drop the defining globals the previous application of state allocated.

Every application of state to the module serializes the live GC data afresh:
`clearApplyState()` forgets which global defines which object, so each object
gets a new defining global, and the ones the previous application allocated
stay in the module. Nothing serialized from then on reads them, and the final
`remove-unused-module-elements` drops them, so the written module is the same;
but until then `applyGlobalsToModule()` scans, sorts and fixes them up on
every application, and every application adds as many again. The cost of an
application grows with the number before it, and the ctors evalled in one
call cost the square of their number.

That is invisible with LLVM's ctors, a handful over a small heap. A module
whose start function builds a heap of 87 000 objects, and which has 284 ctors
to eval, grows by 87 000 globals per application: the 56th ctor took 33 s
where it takes 2 s alone, and the whole call would have taken six hours.
With this the ctors take 1.3 to 2.5 s each and all 284 take nine minutes.

So put back only the defining globals code outside the globals reads -- the
locals a partially evalled ctor resumes with, and the results of a ctor that
kept its export -- and the ones their inits read in turn. Everything else the
previous application allocated is dropped where it is put back.

